-- §4  Every question the absence census asks, the column it claims to read, and what it found.
# One row per question on epistemics/absence_questions.sqlc: the question, the table and column it
# names, and how many rows epistemics/absences.sqlc filed under that question.
#
# Roster and arms are two claims
#   The census is a function from absences to questions, and the roster says which column each
#   question is. reports/integrity.sqlc holds the roster against the catalog, so every column of
#   type `absence_reason` has a question: that is a claim about the roster. Whether each arm reads
#   the column its question names is a claim about the arms, and only attribution reaches it. The
#   rows filed under a question must be exactly as many as the absences filed in its column, and
#   `examples/combinatorics/main.rs` counts the column with a query built from the catalog, which
#   shares nothing with the arm it checks.
#
# Zero against zero is not agreement
#   An arm reading the wrong column of an empty pair would pass, so the program prints the questions
#   with nothing filed apart.
-- epistemics/absence_questions.sqlc, each question counted in epistemics/absences.sqlc.
SELECT q.question AS "question!", q.table_name AS "table_name!", q.column_name AS "column_name!",
       count(a.question) AS "census!"
FROM      (
    :compose(epistemics/absence_questions.sqlc)
) q
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(epistemics/absences.sqlc)
) a ON a.question = q.question
GROUP BY q.question, q.table_name, q.column_name
ORDER BY q.table_name, q.column_name
